---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalorieCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/CalorieCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="calorie-calculator">
  <CalorieCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the calorie calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter sex, height, current weight, goal weight and age.</li>
      <li>
        Pick the activity level that matches your real week. Read the descriptions, not only the labels.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose your rate of change. The rate options are per week; slower is almost always better.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read your daily target, then check the projected timeline below to see when you reach your goal
        weight.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The results update as you type. The whole model runs in this browser tab, and nothing is sent.
    </p>

    <h2>How the daily target is worked out</h2>
    <p>There are three steps. The results panel shows each with your numbers.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>BMR</strong> from the Mifflin-St Jeor equation: the calories you burn at complete rest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Maintenance (TDEE)</strong> = BMR × your activity multiplier. At this intake, your
        weight stays the same.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Target</strong> = maintenance minus (or plus) the daily energy of your chosen rate. A
        kilogram of body fat stores about 7,700 kcal, so a loss of 0.5 kg a week needs a deficit of
        0.5 × 7,700 ÷ 7 ≈ 550 kcal a day.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why the timeline is a curve, not a line</h2>
    <p>
      Many calorie calculators divide the total weight to lose by a fixed weekly rate and show a date.
      That is wrong, and it is why many people think they have "stalled" at week ten when they have
      not.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you get lighter, your maintenance falls: there is less tissue to keep warm and less mass to
      move. If your intake stays fixed, the deficit gets smaller each week, and weight loss slows
      towards an asymptote. This calculator models that: it calculates your BMR and maintenance again
      at each new weight and derives the next week's loss from them. If your intake can never reach the
      goal, the calculator says so and shows no date.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a rate</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Rate</th>
            <th scope="col">Daily deficit</th>
            <th scope="col">Suits</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>0.25 kg / 0.5 lb per week</td>
            <td>about 275 kcal</td>
            <td>Lean people, people who train hard, the last few kilos</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>0.5 kg / 1 lb per week</td>
            <td>about 550 kcal</td>
            <td>The default for most people: visible progress, easy to live with</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>0.75 kg / 1.5 lb per week</td>
            <td>about 825 kcal</td>
            <td>Higher starting body fat, short focused phases</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1 kg / 2 lb per week</td>
            <td>about 1,100 kcal</td>
            <td>Only with a lot to lose, and seldom for more than a few months</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      As a guide, aim for 0.5 to 1 percent of body weight per week. At 100 kg that is 0.5 to 1 kg; at
      60 kg it is 300 to 600 g. Above 1 percent, more of the loss comes from muscle and less from fat.
    </p>

    <h2>Minimum sensible intakes</h2>
    <p>
      Below about 1,500 kcal for men and 1,200 kcal for women, it is difficult to meet protein and
      micronutrient targets, and people stop following the diet. If your chosen rate would put you
      below that, the calculator keeps the target at the floor and tells you. To increase the deficit,
      move more (walking, NEAT, training) and do not eat less.
    </p>
    <p>
      Very low calorie diets below 800 kcal are a medical intervention. They need supervision, and no
      calculator should recommend them.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the scale lies day to day</h2>
    <p>
      Body weight changes by 1 to 2 kg within one day for reasons not related to fat. Glycogen binds
      about 3 g of water per gram stored, so a high-carb day adds water weight. Sodium, hard training,
      menstrual cycle phase, travel and the bathroom all change the number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weigh yourself at the same time each morning and compare seven-day averages. A weekly average
      that has not moved in two to three weeks is a real plateau. One heavy morning is not.
    </p>

    <h2>What to do when progress stops</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Recalculate. After you lose 5 percent of your body weight, your maintenance is lower. Enter your
        new weight above.
      </li>
      <li>
        Measure your intake again. Portions grow over a few weeks for almost everyone. Weigh your food
        for a week and compare.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add movement before you cut food. 2,000 more steps a day is about 80 to 100 kcal and does not
        make you hungrier, as a smaller dinner does.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take a maintenance break. Two weeks at maintenance after a long deficit restores leptin,
        training quality and morale, and makes the next block work better.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
